vms-hass agent @VERSION@ for OpenVMS (@ARCH@)
================================================

Publishes this OpenVMS system to Home Assistant over MQTT: CPU and its modes,
memory, page and swap files, disks, network, processes and users, print and
batch queues, disk errors, shadow sets and cluster membership.  Home Assistant
finds it by MQTT discovery and shows it as a device, with history, dashboards
and automations; "problem" sensors (a stopped queue, a degraded shadow set,
disk errors) are ready for alerts.  The agent connects out to the broker;
nothing listens on this system.  It runs as its own unprivileged account
(NETMBX and TMPMBX only) and refuses to run as SYSTEM or with privileges.

Requires: OpenVMS x86-64 (tested on E9.2-4) or Integrity (tested on
V8.4-2L3), VSI TCP/IP Services, VSI's SSL3 kit, and an MQTT broker that
Home Assistant uses (for example HA's Mosquitto app and MQTT integration).

Setting up (as SYSTEM)
----------------------
1. In Home Assistant, make an MQTT user for the agent.  With the Mosquitto
   app, an HA user (Settings, People, Users) will do.

2. Create the agent's account and set the protections, once:

     $ @HASS$ROOT:[COM]HASS$CONFIGURE

   It asks for the account (default HASS) and, for a new account, its UIC:
   choose a group of its own, above MAXSYSGROUP, that no other account uses
   (e.g. [363,1]; AUTHORIZE SHOW [363,*] lists any).  The account
   gets NETMBX and TMPMBX only and can't log in by any route; the agent still
   runs as it.  Run it again at any time: it changes only an account it
   created.

3. Edit HASS$ROOT:[CONF]AGENT.CONF (made from AGENT.CONF_TEMPLATE, which
   explains every setting).  Two are needed:

     MQTT_HOST=<the broker's address>
     MQTT_USER=<the MQTT user>

   Put the user's password, alone on one line, in HASS$ROOT:[CONF]MQTT.PWD.
   Then run HASS$CONFIGURE again: it makes both files the account's and
   readable by it only (the agent refuses a password file others can read).

   Check what it would publish, without connecting:

     $ AGENT := $HASS$ROOT:[BIN]HASS_AGENT.EXE
     $ AGENT --PRINT

4. Start it:

     $ @HASS$ROOT:[COM]HASS$CONTROL START

   Within a minute the node appears in Home Assistant under Settings,
   Devices & services, MQTT.  STOP, RESTART (after changing AGENT.CONF) and
   STATUS work the same way.

5. At boot and shutdown, add to SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM (after TCP/IP)
   and SYS$MANAGER:SYSHUTDWN.COM:

     $ @SYS$STARTUP:HASS$STARTUP.COM
     $ @SYS$STARTUP:HASS$SHUTDOWN.COM

Without TLS (MQTT_TLS), the password and the readings cross the network
unencrypted, and the agent says so in its log at every start.  To use TLS,
give the broker a certificate (in the Mosquitto app: certfile and keyfile),
set MQTT_TLS=yes, and, for a certificate from your own CA, name the CA's
certificate in MQTT_CA_FILE.

Files
-----
  HASS$ROOT:[BIN]    HASS_AGENT.EXE, and HASS_LAUNCH.EXE (starts the agent as
                     its account)
  HASS$ROOT:[COM]    HASS$CONTROL.COM, HASS$RUN.COM (restarts the agent if it
                     stops), HASS$CONFIGURE.COM
  HASS$ROOT:[CONF]   AGENT.CONF, AGENT.CONF_TEMPLATE, MQTT.PWD
  HASS$ROOT:[DATA]   NODE_ID. (this node's identity in Home Assistant: don't
                     delete it, or HA sees a new device) and PUBLISHED. (the
                     sensors last published)
  HASS$ROOT:[LOG]    HASS_AGENT.LOG (five versions kept)
  SYS$MANAGER:HASS$CONFIG.COM   the account, and whether to start at boot

PRODUCT REMOVE HASS stops the agent and deassigns HASS$ROOT and HASS$USER.
AGENT.CONF, MQTT.PWD, the node ID, the logs, the site settings and the account
are kept, so a reinstall carries on where it left off.  The device stays in
Home Assistant, shown as unavailable; to remove it from HA as well, first:

     $ AGENT := $HASS$ROOT:[BIN]HASS_AGENT.EXE
     $ AGENT --REMOVE

When it doesn't connect, HASS$ROOT:[LOG]HASS_AGENT.LOG says why.  The
messages are explained at https://github.com/issinoho/vms-hass.

